==English==
{{wikipedia}}
{{wikipedia|Amortized analysis}}

===Alternative forms===
* [[amortise]]

===Etymology===
From (the stem of) {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|amortir||to bring to death|lang=frm}}, probably from {{etyl|LL.|en}} *''admortīre'', from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|ad|lang=la}} + {{term|mortem|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/əˈmɔːtɪz/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-amortize.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|amortiz}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[alienate]] (property) in [[mortmain]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To wipe out (a debt, liability etc.) [[gradually]] or in [[installment]]s.
#* '''2002''', {{w|Colin Jones (historian)|Colin Jones}}, ''The Great Nation'', Penguin 2003, p. 318:
#*: extraordinary borrowing had been so extensive, Joly de Fleury reckoned, that even if it were '''amortized''' over the following decade, the state would still be running an annual deficit of over 50 million livres.
# {{context|transitive|computer science|lang=en}} To [[even out]] the [[cost]]s of running an [[algorithm]] over many [[iteration]]s, so that high-cost iterations are much less frequent than low-cost iterations, which lowers the average running time per iteration.

====Antonyms====
* [[accrue]] <!-- to ''increase'' over time, rather than ''decrease'' -->

